The legal texts are acts of the European Union as published by the Publications Office of the European Union in
EUR-Lex and its Cellar repository:

  © European Union, https://eur-lex.europa.eu, 1998-2026.
  Source: Publications Office of the European Union, Cellar (http://publications.europa.eu/resource/celex/),
  retrieved 2 November 2026.

Reuse is authorised under Commission Decision 2011/833/EU of 12 December 2011 on the reuse of Commission documents
(OJ L 330, 14.12.2011, p. 39), which provides:

  Article 6(1): "Documents shall be made available for reuse without application unless otherwise specified and
  without restrictions or, where appropriate, an open licence or disclaimer setting out conditions explaining the
  rights of reusers."

  Article 6(2): "Those conditions, which shall not unnecessarily restrict possibilities for reuse, may include the
  following: the obligation for the reuser to acknowledge the source of the documents; the non-liability of the
  Commission for any consequence stemming from the reuse."

  Article 3: "'reuse' means the use of documents by persons or legal entities of documents, for commercial or
  non-commercial purposes other than the initial purpose for which the documents were produced."

Only the legislation of the European Union published in the paper editions of the Official Journal of the European
Union (or, since 1 July 2013, its authentic electronic edition) is deemed authentic. Consolidated texts are a
documentation tool and have no legal effect; the institutions do not assume any liability for their contents.

Changes made by Lexifina: each version was split into one Markdown file per article, one paragraph or point per line;
spacing, quotation marks and dashes were normalised; editorial references, footnote calls and amendment markers were
left out of the text; and the versions were arranged as git commits dated by the day each change took effect and
credited to the act that made it. The wording of the law was not changed. The European Union and the Publications
Office do not endorse this repository.

Lexifina's own contributions (the arrangement of the history, the commit messages, the tables and the tools) are
dedicated to the public domain under CC0 1.0 Universal:
https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode
